  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-112561",
    "knowledge_point_id": "f7578c48-cc9e-5170-8ffe-5ebee6580677",
    "knowledge_point": "Solving a System Containing Decimals in One Equation",
    "problem": "Let $(x,y)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $x+y?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 \\\\ 0.5x+0.4y=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 &  \\\\ 0.5x+0.4y=0.2 & |\\,\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 \\\\ 5x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the  first equation by $-5,$ then we will get a $-5x$ term that can cancel with the $5x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 & |\\,\\times (-5) \\\\ 5x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-5x-15y=-35 \\\\ 5x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{5x}-15y & =-35 \\\\ \\cancel{5x}+4y & =2 \\\\ (-15y+4y) & =(-33) \\\\ -11y & =-33 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=3$ into the equation $x+3y=7$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+3y & =7 \\\\ x+3(3) & =7 \\\\ x+9 & =7 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2,\\,y=3.$ So,\n\n$$\nx+y=(-2)+(3)=1.\n$$",
